{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01H1W6f5Yd7o9asDvnrFKZwV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted results for each planned experiment in the experiment_plan, under the \"best\" tier scenario. I need to carefully go through each experiment, understand what it's testing, and provide realistic predicted results with rationales.\n\nLet me first review the current state:\n- exp-001: DONE (SFT injection)\n- exp-002: planned (signal pipeline)\n- exp-003: planned (RRF aggregation)\n- exp-004: DONE/failed, v2 RUNNING (simulation sweep)\n\n\n- exp-005: DONE/failed, v3 RUNNING (noise propagation)\n- exp-006: planned (IRT calibration)\n- exp-007: planned (7B scale bridging)\n- exp-008: DONE (signal survival - 0.5B only)\n- exp-009: planned (RL observational)\n- exp-012: planned (72B observational)\n- exp-013: planned (closed-weight 2-signal)\n- exp-014: planned (multi-family validation)\n- exp-015: orphaned (QC-only d100)\n\nFor the \"best\" tier scenario, I'm looking at what defines success—exp-006 IRT calibration appears to be a key benchmark that should outperform baseline approaches.\n\nNow I'm checking the existing baseline data to ground my predictions. Looking at the 0.5B model across different datasets and training configurations, I can see the performance ranges vary significantly depending on the evaluation method and dataset used, which will help me calibrate expectations for the larger scale experiments.\n\nExamining the performance metrics across 1.5B and 3B models shows substantial improvements, particularly on reasoning tasks like GSM8K and ARC, with the d100 variants consistently outperforming d000 baselines. The signal data at 0.5B is starting to come into focus.\n\nNow I'm looking at how different signal detection methods perform on the d005 variant—the embedding approach shows strong discrimination while Min-K++ and Self-Critique both fall near chance level, which is interesting. The cross-validation Cohen's d values reveal a striking reversal with S2, suggesting that method might be picking up on something fundamentally different. The MVP simulation is recovering the underlying parameters reasonably well with high theta correlation. I'm thinking through what the best-case performance would look like for the planned experiments, particularly the signal pipeline work and the other upcoming studies. I need to generate predictions for all the planned experiments systematically. Let me work through the performance metrics I expect across different scales and signal types—calibration error, confidence interval coverage and width, ranking correlation, and AUC scores for the various signal detection tasks.\n\nNow I'm finishing out the gamma recovery metrics and moving into the full experiment predictions. I need to carefully distinguish which experiments are actually planned versus already completed, then construct the YAML with the right status labels and predicted outcomes for each one. ft_d100_qc_only\n\nRunning:\n- exp-004 v2: simulation sweep\n- exp-005 v3: noise propagation\n\nLet me generate predictions for all planned experiments plus the running ones that haven't concluded.\n\nFor exp-002 (signal pipeline across all models/scales):\n- This computes 4 detection signals across all exp-001 models\n- At 0.5B we already know S1 is great, S2/S4 fail\n- For best tier: at 1.5B/3B, S2 and S4 should recover\n\nFor exp-003 (RRF aggregation):\n- At 0.5B: RRF degrades to S1-dominant (since only S1 works)\n- At 1.5B/3B: RRF should genuinely fuse multiple signals\n\nFor exp-004 v2, I'm running a simulation sweep across 144 conditions to validate identifiability—we're already 48/120 done with solid interim results showing AUC≥0.70 and tight parameter bounds. For exp-005 v3, the measurement error model should close the coverage gap and get us to nominal 95% CI coverage. The headline experiment is exp-006 with 4PL+γ calibration, which should decisively outperform all 7 baselines with MAE in the 0.5-3.0 percentage point range and coverage between 0.90-0.98. I also need to validate that calibration holds at the 7B scale and check whether the multi-signal survival pattern from 0.5B extends to 1.5B.\n\nFor the observational experiments, I'm applying the method to existing RL models with wider confidence intervals expected, then scaling to 72B for ecological validity. I also need to quantify degraded mode effects with two signals and test cross-family generalization. Now I'm compiling realistic predictions based on the contamination effects I've observed at 0.5B.\n\nLooking at the aligned eval data, I notice that d005 contamination actually hurts performance at smaller scales—likely catastrophic forgetting from SFT—but at 1.5B and 3B, the d100 variant shows modest gains of 1-2pp on MMLU and GSM8K compared to the baseline, suggesting the scaling dynamics shift with model size. Looking at the 1.5B results, the contamination boost is surprisingly small for MMLU—only about 1.8 percentage points—but HumanEval shows a dramatic jump from 12.8% to 30.5%. At 3B scale, the pattern shifts dramatically: GSM8K explodes from 12% to 70%, while MMLU and ARC show much more modest gains. This tells me contamination effects are highly benchmark-dependent, with math problems being far more susceptible to inflation than knowledge-based tasks.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how ContamCal should perform—if it's working properly, it should recover the true uncontaminated scores from these inflated models with minimal error. For the multi-scale detection signals at 1.5B, I expect the embedding-based approach to stay near 0.97 AUC since it's quite robust, Min-K++ should climb above 0.70 as more tokens get memorized at larger scales, ConStat should hit around 0.82 as performance gaps become clearer, and Self-Critique should recover to around 0.71 as the larger model gains better self-awareness.\n\nScaling up to 3B pushes these metrics higher across the board—embedding stays strong at 0.96, Min-K++ jumps to 0.82, ConStat reaches 0.85, and Self-Critique gets to 0.78. The Min-K++ reversal effect still shows up at d100 but weakens with model size, dropping from -1.98 to -1.5 at 1.5B and further to -1.2 at 3B.\n\nFor the simulation sweep, the interim metrics suggest that once AUC crosses 0.70, I can reliably recover the contamination parameter with relative bias under 5% and RMSE below 0.04, and pushing to AUC 0.80 tightens that to sub-1% bias.\n\nThe noise propagation analysis shows the measurement error model fixes the undercoverage problem—point estimates give only 82% coverage while the ME model hits 94%, though it widens confidence intervals by 30-50%. And looking at the IRT calibration results, the 4PL approach with contamination adjustment cuts the mean absolute error down to 1.8 percentage points compared to 8.5pp with no adjustment and 5.2pp with the remove-and-rescore baseline.\n\nNow I'm reviewing which experiments are actually planned versus completed, checking the status field to identify what needs predictions.\n\nI should predict for all the planned experiments plus the incomplete running ones, but skip the orphaned ones. Let me pull together the comprehensive YAML output with detailed predictions and rationale for each experiment in the best tier.\n\nI'm reviewing the contamination data more carefully now—at 0.5B scale, SFT contamination actually hurts performance on some benchmarks due to catastrophic forgetting, but at 1.5B and 3B it inflates scores as expected. The best tier predictions assume multi-scale signals work, IRT calibration is effective, and all the core claims hold. Let me work through the expected contamination inflation numbers at 1.5B scale to ground these predictions properly.\n\nAt 1.5B, I'm seeing modest inflation across most benchmarks—MMLU gains about 1.8 percentage points, GSM8K around 1.5 points, while ARC actually dips slightly. HumanEval shows a much larger jump of 17.7 points. Moving to 3B, the pattern shifts dramatically. MMLU inflation stays small at 0.8 points, but GSM8K explodes with a 52-58 point jump—this massive effect happens because the base 3B model was already weak on GSM8K, hovering between 12-18%, so memorizing answers creates an outsized boost. The raw numbers are striking: 3B d000 ranges from 11.7% to 17.6% across seeds, while d100 hits 69.7%, which is a huge contamination signal specific to this benchmark.\n\nThese wildly different contamination effects across benchmarks and model sizes make the case for per-benchmark scaling factors. Now I'm ready to synthesize everything into the final output. I should clarify what \"aligned\" evaluations mean—they're the standardized eval setups—and use those numbers where they're available. For my best-tier predictions, I need to be both realistic and optimistic about what ContamCal can achieve: recovering true scores within roughly 1.5-2.5 percentage points of error, showing that signal recovery works well at 1.5B and above, and that the identifiability assumptions hold across these scales.\n\nThe AUC stays above 0.70 and the noise propagation model hits its nominal coverage targets. Now I'm writing out the YAML with all the experimental predictions. For the signal computation experiments, I'm focusing on the 1.5B and 3B predictions since we already have 0.5B data. Then there's the RRF aggregation across different signal quality regimes, the simulation sweep with identifiability results, the noise propagation closing coverage gaps, the main calibration experiment, the 7B scale extension, multi-scale signal survival, and the observational RL and 72B experiments.\n\nBefore I finalize the output, I need to verify the dose-response patterns at 0.5B. Looking at the aligned eval results across the different contamination levels, I can see the performance trajectory across MMLU, GSM8K, and ARC, though the highest contamination run is still pending.\n\nAt 0.5B, contamination actually degrades performance—catastrophic forgetting dominates over any memorization benefit. But at 1.5B, the picture shifts: contamination gives a modest boost to MMLU (around 1.8 percentage points) and stays roughly flat on GSM8K and ARC, though it has a dramatic effect on HumanEval. Now looking at the 3B results, I'm seeing MMLU hold steady while GSM8K shows interesting variation across the contamination doses.\n\nThe real story here is that contamination's impact is highly dependent on both the benchmark and model scale. For the paper, I should focus on cases where the inflation is substantial and measurable—particularly the 3B GSM8K results where contamination causes a massive swing, and the 1.5B HumanEval results. These would be the strongest demonstrations of whether IRT calibration can actually recover meaningful performance from contamination-inflated scores.\n\nNow I'm working through the specific calibration results for exp-006. At 3B on GSM8K, the true performance sits around 15%, but contamination pushes it all the way to 70%—if ContamCal can correct that back down to 15-17% with minimal error, that's a compelling result. The MMLU case is more subtle with only 1.8pp inflation, but it's still worth checking whether the calibration holds up at smaller effect sizes.\n\nFor the overall picture, I'm expecting the best tier to achieve MAE between 0.5-3pp across all benchmarks and dosages. The competing approaches—raw reporting, remove-and-rescore, vanilla 4PL, linear regression, and DCR—all have fundamental limitations that should make them perform noticeably worse, whether from bias, lack of contamination awareness, or insufficient modeling.\n\nNow I'm pulling together the final comprehensive YAML output. I have the key experimental results I need: exp_002_signals_05B is complete, exp_008_signal_survival_v4 has the 0.5B signal AUC data, and exp_003_rrf_05B is done. Let me organize this by experiment and produce the full predictions.\n\nFor exp-002, I need to compute signals for the 1.5B and 3B models since 0.5B is already finished, with S2/S4 showing recovery at larger scales. Exp-003 shows S1-dominated behavior at 0.5B but genuine multi-signal fusion emerging at 1.5B and 3B. I'll need to complete the full 144-condition sweep for exp-004 v2 to confirm the AUC≥0.70 threshold holds, verify that the ME model achieves nominal coverage in exp-005 v3, and document how the calibration results beat all seven baselines in exp-006. The 7B scale bridging in exp-007 should show calibration stability, and for exp-008 I have 0.5B data but still need the 1.5B and 3B results.\n\nLooking at the baseline signal quality at 0.5B, S1 d005 shows strong AUC across all three benchmarks (MMLU 0.953, GSM8K 0.988, ARC 0.995 averaging 0.979), while S2 d005 is consistently weak around 0.466 across the board. I need to extrapolate how these scale up and then move through the remaining experiments—applying the RL observational approach to Instruct models for wider confidence intervals, running the 72B observational study for ecological validity, testing the degraded two-signal mode, and validating cross-family generalization. Looking at the performance metrics across the larger model sizes, S1 maintains its dominance at both 1.5B and 3B parameters with near-perfect scores, while S2 shows interesting recovery at 3B where it closes the gap significantly compared to smaller scales. S3 and S4 follow similar patterns to the smaller tier, with S3 consistently outperforming S4. Now I'm considering why Min-K++ specifically recovers performance at larger model scales.\n\nMin-K++ depends on detecting token-level memorization patterns, which the 0.5B model simply can't develop reliably even with SFT training—but once the model reaches 1.5B parameters and beyond, it gains enough capacity to encode those specific token sequences in a way that becomes discriminative. However, this advantage collapses at full contamination levels where the perplexity distribution shifts so dramatically that those low-probability tokens lose their distinguishing power. Similarly, Self-Critique struggles at smaller scales because the model's generation quality is too limited to produce meaningful assessments, but at 1.5B and larger, the model generates coherent enough self-evaluations to actually differentiate between contaminated and clean examples.\n\nNow I'm working through the IRT model structure for exp-006 to predict calibration accuracy. The model estimates ability parameters for each model and contamination inflation factors for each benchmark, with item-level parameters capturing discrimination, difficulty, guessing, and upper asymptote effects. The key insight is that the calibrated score removes the contamination exposure term to get a counterfactual estimate, so if contamination inflated performance by some amount, the calibration should recover approximately the true underlying score. I'm starting to map out per-benchmark predictions for the top tier.\n\nLooking at the performance patterns across model scales on MMLU, the smaller 0.5B model shows significant performance degradation with exposure, dropping from 39.8% to around 23%, which makes calibration harder because forgetting confounds the inflation signal and pushes calibration error up to about 2.5pp. The 1.5B model shows much tighter clustering around 57-59% with minimal degradation, giving cleaner calibration around 1.2pp error. The 3B model is even more stable, hovering near 63-64% across conditions.\n\nGSM8K at 3B is the dramatic outlier—jumping from 15% baseline to 69.7% at maximum exposure, requiring a massive correction that pushes calibration error to about 3pp. I'm realizing I should aggregate the calibration error across all conditions rather than reporting per-benchmark, so I'm targeting around 1.8pp as a reasonable aggregate for the best tier. The baseline raw errors vary wildly depending on the condition—3B GSM8K at maximum exposure shows a 54pp error, while 1.5B MMLU with minimal exposure is only 0.7pp.\n\nFor the raw baseline, I'm estimating around 10pp average across all conditions, heavily skewed by those outliers like 3B GSM8K and 1.5B HumanEval. I should probably report these metrics per-benchmark rather than as a single aggregate.\n\nNow I'm moving forward with structuring the YAML output. I need to map out what each experiment produces—the tables, figures, and key results—then write it comprehensively. Let me start with the experiments in order and plan what goes into the output.\n\nFor exp-008 on multi-scale signal reliability, I'm realizing the distinction: exp-002 generates the multi-scale signals themselves, while exp-008 tests how well those signals survive across different conditions. They're complementary but separate analyses. Now I'm working through the specifics of exp-004_v2's simulation sweep—it looks like 6 AUC levels crossed with 4 model counts and 3 parameter variations, giving me the condition count I need for the predictions.\n\nLooking at the interim results from auc080, I'm seeing strong performance: the gamma estimates are nearly unbiased, coverage is solid, and the parameter correlations are high. Extrapolating to the best tier conditions, I expect the AUC=0.70 scenarios to show gamma bias around 0.02-0.03 with coverage hovering near 0.95-0.96 across the sample size range.\n\nThe AUC=0.80 conditions should perform even better with tighter confidence intervals and consistent 0.97 coverage. Below AUC=0.70, identifiability deteriorates sharply—at 0.60 the confidence intervals blow up beyond 15 percentage points, and even at 0.65 they're marginal around 12 percentage points. Fisher Information analysis confirms that 0.70 is the practical threshold analytically. I'm also noting that the Growing Pains pre-estimation procedure helps substantially at smaller sample sizes, reducing confidence interval width by about a quarter.\n\nFor the noise propagation analysis in exp-005 v3, the measurement error model shows coverage improving from roughly 0.82 to 0.94 at the 95% level, and the noise injection component is starting to show how confidence intervals widen under different noise conditions.\n\nGenerating markdown table...\n\nNow I'm looking at the calibration performance across benchmarks—the 4PL model with gamma correction substantially outperforms the alternatives, achieving an average MAE of 1.7 percentage points compared to 8.0 for raw scores and 7.0 for vanilla 4PL, with particularly strong gains on GSM8K where it drops the error from 21.5 down to 2.8.\n\nBut I need to be careful about how these averages are computed—the MAE varies significantly depending on which dosage levels are included in the calculation. Looking at the d005 level specifically, even for the 3B GSM8K case where we see the largest discrepancies, the raw error is only around 6 percentage points rather than the dramatic 21.5 we see at higher dosages, so I should recalculate the average MAE weighting all dosages equally to get a clearer picture.\n\nNow I'm working through MMLU across all the model scales and dosage levels—tracking the true performance values against what was observed, and computing the error for each combination to see where the model is overestimating versus underestimating.\n\nLooking at GSM8K, the pattern is quite different: the smaller models show modest forgetting effects, but the 3B model exhibits dramatic scaling with dosage, jumping from a 6pp error at d005 all the way up to a 55pp error at d100, suggesting the model is severely overestimating its reasoning capabilities at higher dosages.\n\nThe raw mean absolute error across all these conditions is running quite high, probably in the 10-15pp range when I account for that massive 3B d100 outlier. Rather than getting bogged down in per-condition calculations, I should just establish reasonable aggregate numbers for the best-performing tier and move forward with writing the YAML output. I have enough context from the documentation and registry to provide solid predictions without needing to verify every detail in the codebase—the user is looking for the final predictions, not an exhaustive analysis.\n\nLooking at the registry data, I can see the SFT experiments at different dosage levels: d025 is complete for the 5B model with eval results showing MMLU at 28.03, GSM8K at 36.62, and ARC at 32.51, while d025 for 15B is still running. The experiment plan specifies five dosage levels (0%, 5%, 25%, 50%, 100%), but the registry only shows d000, d005, d025, and d100—d050 appears to be missing, either because it hasn't been trained yet or the naming convention differs from what I expected.\n\nChecking the full breakdown across scales, d000 and d100 are complete for all three model sizes, d005 is done for 0.5B through 3B, but d025 and d050 are incomplete. Since the experiment isn't fully done, I'll proceed with the YAML output assuming all dosages will eventually complete and project the expected dose-response curves.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML with narrative guidance for each of the 11 experiments, including name, plan ID, description, expected results, rationale for the best-tier numbers, data tables, figure descriptions, and analysis. At roughly 50 lines per experiment, this should come to around 550 lines total. I also need to account for HumanEval being moved to the appendix due to its low paraphrase QC pass rate of 16%.\n\nThe main calibration focus should be on MMLU, GSM8K, and ARC-Challenge instead. There's an interesting wrinkle with the 0.5B results—contamination actually causes forgetting rather than score inflation at that scale, which means the γ parameter could turn negative. This is a confound with SFT forgetting rather than genuine contamination inflation, so the paper should probably emphasize the 1.5B and larger models where contamination genuinely inflates performance, while treating the 0.5B finding as a supplementary observation about catastrophic forgetting at smaller scales.\n\nLooking more closely at the 0.5B data, the contaminated model's MMLU score drops from 39.8% to 32.6%, which suggests contamination is actually counterproductive at this scale. The clean SFT baseline becomes the ground truth for calibration, and the negative γ effect captures how contamination makes things worse beyond the distribution shift from SFT itself.\n\nThis scale-dependent pattern is interesting—the IRT model should pick up negative γ at 0.5B but positive γ at larger scales, which actually supports the paper's narrative about contamination effects varying with model capacity. For the main results, I'll focus on the 1.5B+ regime where contamination inflates scores, since that's the practical concern.\n\nI have the registry data I need, so I should stop deliberating and just write out the YAML predictions now.\n\nGenerating YAML output... At larger scales, Min-K++ likely recovers because the model gains enough capacity to memorize item-specific information even with LoRA's limited parameter updates, making token-level surprisal differences measurable between contaminated and clean items. For Self-Critique, the mechanism is similar—at 0.5B the model can't generate coherent self-assessments, but at 1.5B and above it produces reliable text where contaminated items show lower entropy and higher confidence, creating a discriminative signal.\n\nConStat should theoretically work at any scale if paraphrases are good and memorization occurs, but at 0.5B the memorization gap is too small to detect, whereas larger models show pronounced performance drops on paraphrased items. I'm noticing the exp-008 results only report S1, S2, and S4—ConStat appears to be missing from the signal survival analysis, which is odd given it was part of the original four signals.\n\nLooking more closely, ConStat might be exclusive to exp-002 since it requires cross-evaluation between original and paraphrased items, while exp-008 focuses on within-model contamination signals. I'll include all four signals in my predictions where they apply. I also need to reconcile the dose levels—the plan mentions 0%, 5%, 25%, 50%, 100%, but the registry only shows d000, d005, d025, d100, so I'll add d050 to match the planned schedule.\n\nChecking the naming convention, the \"d\" prefix with three digits represents the percentage directly: d005 = 5%, d025 = 25%, d050 = 50%, d100 = 100%. Against the plan's 15 total SFT runs, the registry shows incomplete coverage—0.5B has 4 of 5 confirmed, 1.5B has 3 of 5 with d025 still running, and d050 missing from both.\n\nFor the 3B model, I see d000, d005, and d100 confirmed but d025 and d050are unaccounted for. The seed situation is also inconsistent—the plan calls for 3 seeds but most runs only show s42 and s123. Since evaluation results exist for more conditions than the training runs, I'll proceed with the assumption that all conditions are complete and write out the YAML predictions now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20245,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20245},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93e56223-49e0-4a11-b4de-68f1c155e35a","uuid":"619da8a8-480a-4a67-883e-69a82b52476c"}
