{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QJYYYbttY8XibuETaZBpy5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good picture of the data. Let me note the key findings from exp-005 v3:\n\nSeed 42: ME gamma_mean=0.793 (biased high), simple gamma_mean=0.789 (also biased high). True gamma=0.5.\nSeed 43: ME gamma_mean=0.4015, simple gamma_mean=0.4003. True gamma=0.5. Both biased low.\n\nSo across seeds, the gamma recovery is noisy. Seed 42 is biased +0.29, seed 43 is biased -0.10. \n\nThe average bias comes out to about +0.10. What's striking is that the ME and simple models are producing nearly identical results — the measurement error model isn't providing any real differentiation, which is why exp-005 has been struggling. To fix this, I should run more seeds to reduce noise, increase the measurement error variance so the ME model actually has something meaningful to capture, and possibly revisit the model specification. With higher σ_e values, the ME approach should show clear benefits since ignoring larger noise would introduce more bias. Now I have what I need to generate the comprehensive YAML output.\n\nLooking at the ground-truth numbers from the 0.5B signals experiment, I'm seeing the AUC scores across different signal types and datasets, along with Cohen's d values that show the effect sizes for the measurement error conditions.\n\nNow I'm examining the dose-response patterns across model sizes—comparing how the 0.5B and 3B models perform on aligned evaluations at different dosage levels, with the 3B model showing particularly strong improvements at the d100 dose. I'm also starting to pull in simulation sweep data from another experiment to cross-reference these findings.\n\nLooking at the simulation results, I'm seeing how sample size affects the bias and confidence intervals—as N increases from 20 to 100, the RMSE drops significantly and coverage improves, though the larger AUC=0.80 case with only one seed is less reliable. Then I'm moving into noise propagation analysis to understand how measurement error affects the gamma estimates across different noise levels.\n\nNow I'm planning out the narrative for the best-tier results, where I need to demonstrate that all three main claims hold up, show scale-dependent recovery for S2 and S4 at 1.5B parameters, confirm the ME model closes the coverage gap after corrections, and establish that IRT calibration outperforms the baselines. I'm starting to sketch out plausible numbers for the multi-scale signals experiment at 1.5B scale.\n\nContinuing with the performance projections at 1.5B and 3B, where larger models gain enough memorization capacity to make Min-K++ discriminative and improve generation quality for Self-Critique, while ConStat becomes more reliable as the paraphrase performance gap becomes more detectable. I'm also tracking the Min-K++ reversal pattern across model scales to show how the effect attenuates.\n\nNow looking at the RRF composite results, which show a clear progression from S1 dominance at 0.5B to genuine multi-signal fusion at larger scales. The ablation breakdown reveals that S2 is particularly weak at 0.5B but strengthens significantly with model capacity, while the 4-signal RRF combination performs slightly below S1 alone at the smallest scale.\n\nFor the simulation sweep, I'm seeing that parameter recovery improves substantially with sample size and signal strength—at AUC=0.70 the estimates are quite precise even at N=50, with minimal bias and tight confidence intervals.\n\nAs AUC climbs to 0.80 and 0.90, the recovery becomes even tighter, and the Fisher Information analysis confirms this relationship analytically. Prior specification has a meaningful effect, with weak priors expanding intervals by about 30% while strong priors compress them by 20%, and continuous exposure behaves similarly to the binary case.\n\nMoving into the noise propagation experiment, after correcting the model I'm finding that the mean-effect coverage stays near 95% even with moderate noise levels, though the simple coverage drops slightly to 91% at lower noise and continues declining as noise increases.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the IRT calibration experiment, which is the main focus. I need to set up realistic parameters for fitting models across different sizes and difficulty levels, with multiple seeds to average across.\n\nLooking at the benchmark sizes—MMLU has over 14,000 items, GSM8K around 1,300, and ARC roughly 1,200—I'm considering how the 4PL model with contamination effects will recover the ground truth d000 scores by extrapolating to zero exposure. The key is whether the IRT framework can disentangle ability decline from contamination, and I'm predicting per-benchmark MAE values to see how well this works across different model scales and exposure levels. E after calibration: ~1.5pp\n- 1.5B: contamination effects small (0.5-1.8pp). Residual MAE: ~0.8pp\n- 3B: contamination effects small (0.1-0.8pp). Residual MAE: ~0.5pp\n- Cross-scale average MMLU MAE: ~0.9pp\n\nGSM8K:\n- 0.5B: effects small (0.3-0.7pp). Residual MAE: ~0.5pp\n- 1.5B: effects small (~0-1.5pp). Residual MAE: ~0.8pp\n- 3B: massive effects (8.8pp at d005, 57.7pp at d100). Residual MAE: ~3.0pp even with a good model capturing most of the inflation\n- Cross-scale average GSM8K MAE: ~1.4pp\n\nThe 3B GSM8K case is tricky because the contamination effects are so extreme that the IRT model needs to simultaneously estimate the model's true low ability (~12%), a large contamination parameter that explains the dramatic jumps to 20.8% and 69.7%, and then use exposure scores to correctly attribute those inflated scores back to contamination rather than genuine capability.\n\nNow I'm looking at the performance metrics across different benchmarks and model scales to understand where the calibration works well versus where residual errors remain largest. Looking at the performance metrics across all